```

Save the file with `Cmd + S`.

**Security note:** `.gitignore` is a safety measure, not a guarantee that every credential is protected. Before making the first commit, inspect your staged files and check for hardcoded Sauce Labs access keys.

If you previously pasted a real Sauce Labs key into a script, replace it with `process.env.SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY` and rotate that key if it was exposed.

## Step 6. Create your master README

Every GitHub repository should have a `README.md` explaining what the repository contains.

Create it:

```bash
touch README.md
code README.md
```

Paste:

<WritingBlock id="59341" variant="document"># Sauce Labs Testing

A centralized collection of automated testing projects, examples, and experiments using Sauce Labs.

## Purpose

This repository serves as a personal testing workspace for practicing and developing automation across multiple languages and frameworks.

The goal is to maintain organized, reusable test projects while exploring the capabilities of the Sauce Labs platform.

## Testing Frameworks

| Framework | Purpose | Languages |
|---|---|---|
| Appium | Android and iOS mobile automation | JavaScript, Python, Java, C#, Ruby |
| Selenium | Cross-browser web automation | JavaScript, Python, Java, C#, Ruby |
| Playwright | Modern browser automation | JavaScript, Python |
| Cypress | End-to-end web testing | JavaScript |
| API Testing | API validation and integration testing | JavaScript, Python |

## Repository Structure

Each framework has its own directory, divided into language-specific projects.

Each project may include:

- Test scripts
- Framework configuration
- Dependency files
- Setup instructions
- Sample test data
- Troubleshooting notes

## Current Projects

### Appium — JavaScript

Automated Android testing using WebdriverIO and Sauce Labs Real Device Cloud.

**Application:** Sauce Labs My Demo App (React Native)

Initial test coverage includes product catalog validation, product details, cart functionality, login validation, locked-out users, and checkout.

## Getting Started

1. Clone this repository.
2. Navigate to the appropriate framework and language folder.
3. Install the required dependencies.
4. Configure your Sauce Labs credentials using environment variables.
5. Follow the project-specific README to execute tests.

## Sauce Labs Credentials

Tests use environment variables for authentication:

- `SAUCE_USERNAME`
- `SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY`

Never commit access keys or other secrets to this repository.

## Future Plans

- Expand Android and iOS Appium coverage.
- Add Selenium tests in multiple languages.
- Add Playwright and Cypress projects.
- Build reusable test utilities.
- Integrate GitHub Actions with Sauce Labs.
- Document debugging and troubleshooting examples.

## Resources

- [Sauce Labs Documentation](https://docs.saucelabs.com/)
- [Appium Documentation](https://appium.io/docs/en/latest/)
- [Selenium Documentation](https://www.selenium.dev/documentation/)
- [Playwright Documentation](https://playwright.dev/)
- [Cypress Documentation](https://docs.cypress.io/)</WritingBlock>

This will serve as the homepage of your GitHub repository.

You can update it as you add new frameworks, languages, and example applications.

## Step 7. Make the empty folders visible on GitHub

Git only tracks files, not empty directories.

To ensure all your framework folders appear in GitHub, we can add `.gitkeep` placeholder files.

From the repository root, run:

```bash
find appium selenium playwright cypress api-testing shared docs \
  -type d -empty -exec touch {}/.gitkeep \;
```

This adds an empty `.gitkeep` file to each currently empty directory.

Once you add actual test files to a folder, you can remove its `.gitkeep` file.

## Step 8. Create a project-specific README for Appium JavaScript

The master README describes the overall repository. Each testing project should also have its own README.

Create one:

```bash
touch appium/javascript/README.md
code appium/javascript/README.md
```

Add:

<WritingBlock id="59342" variant="document"># Appium — JavaScript

Automated Android testing using WebdriverIO, Appium, and Sauce Labs.

## Application Under Test

Sauce Labs My Demo App — React Native

Application build: `Android-MyDemoAppRN.1.3.0.build-244.apk`

## Requirements

- Node.js and npm
- Sauce Labs account
- Sauce Labs Real Device Cloud access
- Demo app uploaded to Sauce Labs App Storage

## Installation

From this directory:

```bash
npm install
```

## Configure Credentials

Set your Sauce Labs username and access key in your local environment:

```bash
export SAUCE_USERNAME="your-username"
export SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY="your-access-key"
```

## Run Tests

Run the product catalog test:

```bash
node sauce-demo-app-tests.js catalog
```

Run the shopping cart test:

```bash
node sauce-demo-app-tests.js cart
```

Run the checkout test:

```bash
node sauce-demo-app-tests.js checkout
```

Run the full test suite:

```bash
node sauce-demo-app-tests.js all
```

## Available Tests

| Test | Description |
|---|---|
| catalog | Validate product catalog |
| product | Validate product details |
| cart | Test cart functionality |
| locked | Test locked-out user authentication |
| validation | Test required-field login validation |
| checkout | Complete an end-to-end checkout |

## Sauce Labs Configuration

- Platform: Android
- Automation driver: UiAutomator2
- Device selection: Google Android devices
- Region: US West
- Application source: Sauce Labs App Storage

## Debugging

Test results and session artifacts are available in the Sauce Labs Automated Test Results dashboard.

If a test fails, review the Appium command log, available device logs, screenshots, and session video.</WritingBlock>

Now your JavaScript Appium project is independently documented.

