Click **Create repository**.

We don't want GitHub to generate a README or `.gitignore` because we've already created those locally.

Once the repository is created, leave that page open.

## Step 10. Make your first Git commit

Return to your Terminal.

Verify you're in the repository root:

```bash
cd ~/Developer/sauce-labs/sauce-labs-testing
```

Check the Git status:

```bash
git status
```

Before committing, make sure none of your projects contains credentials or other files you don't intend to publish.

You can also verify that dependencies and environment files are ignored:

```bash
git check-ignore appium/javascript/node_modules \
  appium/javascript/.env
```

Stage your project:

```bash
git add .
```

Review exactly what will be included:

```bash
git diff --cached --stat
git diff --cached --name-only
```

Look through the staged changes, especially JavaScript test files and configuration files, for secrets.

If everything looks correct, create your first commit:

```bash
git commit -m "Initial Sauce Labs testing monorepo setup"
```

## Step 11. Connect your local repository to GitHub

On your new GitHub repository page, copy the HTTPS repository URL.

It should look something like:

```text
https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/sauce-labs-testing.git
```

Replace `YOUR_USERNAME` in the following command with your actual GitHub username:

```bash
git remote add origin \
  https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/sauce-labs-testing.git
```

Verify:

```bash
git remote -v
```

You should see `origin` pointing to your GitHub repository.

Then push the initial commit:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

GitHub may require authentication. If you have GitHub CLI installed and authenticated, you can use that; otherwise, use your existing Git credential configuration or a personal access token when prompted. GitHub does not accept a normal account password for Git operations over HTTPS.

Refresh your repository page.

You should now see your full testing project structure on GitHub.

## Step 12. Understand your daily workflow

From here onward, you can work on each testing project independently.

For example, to work on Appium JavaScript:

```bash
cd ~/Developer/sauce-labs/sauce-labs-testing/appium/javascript
```

Run your tests:

```bash
node sauce-demo-app-tests.js catalog
```

After modifying or adding test scripts, return to the monorepo root:

```bash
cd ../..
```

Commit the changes:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Expand Appium JavaScript tests"
git push
```

You can do the same thing for your Python Selenium project or any other testing framework.

### Example: Adding a Python Selenium project

Navigate to the Python Selenium directory:

```bash
cd ~/Developer/sauce-labs/sauce-labs-testing/selenium/python
```

Create a virtual environment:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
```

Install your testing dependencies:

```bash
python -m pip install selenium pytest
```

Create a test file:

```bash
touch test_login.py
```

You can then build out that project without interfering with your JavaScript Appium setup.

The `.venv` directory will remain local and won't be committed.

## Step 13. Verify the complete setup

Here's a checklist you can use while following the instructions.

{@body const steps = ["Created sauce-labs-testing folder","Initialized Git on main","Created framework and language directories","Opened the project in VS Code","Copied existing Appium JavaScript project","Created .gitignore","Created master README.md","Created Appium JavaScript README.md","Added .gitkeep files","Created private GitHub repository","Reviewed and committed local files","Connected origin and pushed main"]}
{@body const [checked,setChecked] = DIL.useState({})}
<box border radius="xl" padding={3} gap={3}>
  <row align="center" justify="between">
    **Monorepo setup checklist**
    <text color="secondary" size="xs" tabularNums>{Object.values(checked).filter(Boolean).length} / {steps.length}</text>
  </row>
  <box background="surface-tertiary" radius="full" height="6px" clip>
    <box background="rgba(32,136,104,1)" height="100%" width={`${Object.values(checked).filter(Boolean).length/steps.length*100}%`}/>
  </box>
  {#each steps as s,i}
    <checkbox key={i} checked={!!checked[i]} onChange={v=>setChecked(p=>({...p,[i]:v}))}>{s}</checkbox>
  {/each}
  <row justify="end">
    <button color="secondary" variant="outline" size="sm" onClick={()=>setChecked({})}>Reset checklist</button>
  </row>
</box>

## What your finished repository will look like

Your GitHub homepage will show one repository with an organized structure:

```text
sauce-labs-testing/
│
├── appium/
│   ├── javascript/       ← Your current demo app tests
│   ├── python/
│   ├── java/
│   ├── csharp/
│   └── ruby/
│
├── selenium/
│   ├── javascript/
│   ├── python/
│   ├── java/
│   ├── csharp/
│   └── ruby/
│
├── playwright/
│   ├── javascript/
│   └── python/
│
├── cypress/
│   └── javascript/
│
├── api-testing/
│   ├── javascript/
│   └── python/
│
├── shared/
├── docs/
├── .github/
│   └── workflows/
│
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

**My recommendation for what to do after this:** Add GitHub Actions to run specific Sauce Labs test suites automatically. We can configure independent workflows so your Appium tests, Selenium tests, and Playwright tests run only when relevant files change, while keeping everything in one central repository.